Binary Search On Answer / KOKO Type
====================================

Binary Search On Answer
-----------------------
 -- Sometimes, we overlook the possibility of using binary search as a solution to a problem,
 -- especially when we see that the given array is unsorted.

 -- Let's consider a well-known problem called "KOKO Eating Banana" to illustrate this concept.
 -- (Please take a moment to familiarize yourself with this problem before continuing with the discussion.)
 -- These types of problems, including the "KOKO Eating Banana" problem, are commonly referred to as "Binary Search on Answer" problems.
 -- To make it easier to remember within our coding group, we simply call them "koko type problems."

 -- By using binary search on the answer, the algorithm avoids iterating through all possible eating
 -- speeds and instead uses a divide-and-conquer approach to converge on the optimal solution more quickly.
 -- It repeatedly adjusts the search range by comparing the result of the 'ispossible' function
 -- and updating the 'start' and 'end' variables accordingly.


 -- Binary search on answer is a powerful technique that can greatly reduce the time complexity of
 -- searching for optimal or specific values in a problem, particularly when the values are within
 -- a known range or can be compared to a specific criterion.

 -- It is widely used in various problem-solving scenarios to optimize search algorithms and
 -- find efficient solutions.


